"""Swimming and wushu: the objectives that are pure arithmetic or a rule read in full (Nov 2026). Both frameworks
had zero drills.

Swimming draws on World Aquatics rules (`world-aquatics-rules`, read in full) for the legal/illegal and relay items,
and on ordinary race and training arithmetic for the rest (pace, splits, clean-swimming velocity, stroke rate and
distance per cycle, stroke index, critical swim speed, work:rest, taper volume). Every number is recomputed.

Wushu is deliberately thin: the IWUF rulebook was read only at crosswalk level, so the difficulty-code and deduction
tables are NOT drilled — writing those values from memory would be inventing data. Only the structural rules are
drilled (how a 10-point score splits, how a judging panel's marks are trimmed and averaged), and any rule a card
needs beyond that is printed on the card itself, the way the ASHRAE clean-air items handle unread table values."""
from opl_dsl import Q

FILE = "ath-drills"
PRACTICE = "P1"
MC = ["{{o1}}", "{{o2}}", "{{o3}}", "{{o4}}"]
SWR, SWP, SWT = "swim.rules", "swim.pacing", "swim.training"
WSJ = "wushu.judging"


def facts(name, skill, rows, explain, pace="recall"):
    return Q(name, skill, "{{q}}", pace=pace, cases=[{"q": q, "o1": a, "o2": b, "o3": c, "o4": d} for q, a, b, c, d in rows],
             choice=MC, correct="0", verify="authored", explain=explain)


